@h1 Multi-file manuscripts h1@

A book does not want to live in one enormous file. Wit lets you split a long
work into per-chapter files and assemble them in order, with shared definitions —
your citation format, your schema, your recurring values — living in one place
that every chapter can draw on.

@h2 Why split h2@

Per-chapter files mean sane editing, small diffs, and the ability to reorder or
hold back a chapter without touching the rest. And a shared vocabulary file keeps
one definition of each source and format across the whole manuscript, so nothing
drifts out of sync between chapters.

@h2 Pulling in another file h2@

A directive at the top of a file imports another file's definitions, data, and
nodes. You write the keyword and a relative path:

@@@pre
reference ./shared/sources.wit
reference ./shared/schema.wit
pre@@@

After that, everything defined in those files is available in this one — as if
you had typed it here.

@aside(class note) 💡 The import keyword is only special at the very start of a
line, followed by a path. That is why you will only ever see it in a code block:
in ordinary prose the same word is just a word. aside@

@h2 A shared vocabulary h2@

Put the things every chapter needs — sources, a citation format, a schema for a
recurring table — in a small set of shared files:

@@@pre
shared/sources.wit     ~ #weil, #berger, … named once
shared/schema.wit      ~ the column schema for a recurring table
pre@@@

Each chapter imports them and uses the names. Define once, use in every chapter.

@h2 Wrapping a chapter for assembly h2@

Wrap each chapter's content in a block definition so the master file can place it
where it wants:

@@@pre
#chapter_one
@h2 Chapter One h2@

The road climbed for an hour before the house appeared.
chapter_one#
pre@@@

The chapter file defines the node; it does not emit anything on its own.

@h2 The master file h2@

A master file imports the parts and emits them in the order you choose. The order
of the uses _is_ the order of the book:

@@@pre
reference ./chapters/one.wit
reference ./chapters/two.wit

@chapter_one chapter_one@
@chapter_two chapter_two@
pre@@@

Reorder the two uses and the chapters reorder. Add a third and it slots in. The
master file is the table of contents made executable.

@h2 An honest status note h2@

Multi-file assembly is real and works, but two of the example projects shipped in
this repository currently _fail_ to build — one from a comma inside a record
field value, one from an unresolved reference in an additive-partial demo. If you
are learning from the examples folder, build a file before trusting it, and reach
for the smaller, verified pieces first. The mechanics on this page are sound; some
of the older sample projects need fixing.

@h2 Common mistakes h2@

@ul
@li _A comma inside a shared record value._ The same comma rule that breaks a
single-file record breaks a shared one. Keep field values comma-free. li@
@li _A relative path that does not resolve._ Import paths are relative to the
importing file; check the path if a name comes back unresolved. li@
@li _Expecting a chapter file to render on its own._ A file that only _defines_ a
chapter emits nothing until a master file _uses_ it. li@
ul@

